// heartsim tutorial: atrial arrhythmias. Original text by the heartsim authors,
// MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see ASSETS-LICENSES.md). Format: see crates/heartsim-core/src/tutorial.rs.
Tutorial(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "06_atrial",
    title: "Atrial arrhythmias",
    summary: "Premature atrial beats (conducted and blocked), atrial tachycardia, the sick sinus syndrome, atrial flutter with its AV ratios, and atrial fibrillation with cardioversion.",
    references: [
        "Hindricks G et al. 2020 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of atrial fibrillation. Eur Heart J 2021;42:373-498.",
        "Joglar JA et al. 2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS guideline for the diagnosis and management of atrial fibrillation. Circulation 2024;149:e1-e156.",
        "Kusumoto FM et al. 2018 ACC/AHA/HRS guideline on the evaluation and management of patients with bradycardia and cardiac conduction delay. Circulation 2019;140:e382-e482.",
        "Goldberger AL, Goldberger ZD, Shvilkin A. Goldberger's Clinical Electrocardiography: A Simplified Approach, 9th ed. Elsevier 2017 (ch. 12, 15).",
        "Moe GK, Abildskov JA. Atrial fibrillation as a self-sustaining arrhythmia independent of focal discharge. Am Heart J 1959;58:59-70.",
        "Burns E, Buttner R. Life in the Fast Lane ECG Library: premature atrial complexes, atrial tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, sick sinus syndrome. litfl.com/ecg-library.",
        "Brugada J et al. 2019 ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with supraventricular tachycardia. Eur Heart J 2020;41:655-720.",
        "Burns E, Buttner R. Life in the Fast Lane ECG Library: atrial flutter. litfl.com/ecg-library.",
    ],
    pages: [
        Page(
            title: "Premature atrial contractions",
            markdown: r#"
A **premature atrial contraction (PAC)** is a beat that fires **early** from somewhere in the
atria. On the strip:

* an **early P wave** whose shape differs from the sinus P wave (it starts elsewhere),
* usually a **narrow QRS** that follows it,
* a pause afterwards that is **shorter than twice the normal beat interval**, because the PAC
  resets the sinus node.

PACs are common at any age and are usually harmless. Caffeine, stress and lack of sleep make them
more frequent. Here every third beat is a PAC.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("pac_frequent")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "How can you tell an early beat is atrial rather than ventricular?",
                choices: [
                    "It is preceded by an early P wave and has a narrow QRS",
                    "It has a wide, bizarre QRS and no P wave",
                    "It comes after a long pause",
                    "It has a delta wave",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "An early P wave with a normal, narrow QRS is a premature atrial beat. A premature ventricular beat is wide and has no preceding P wave.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "A PAC that is blocked",
            markdown: r#"
A PAC that comes **very early** may arrive while the AV node is still **refractory** from the
previous beat. The atria contract, but the impulse cannot cross the node: a P wave appears with **no
QRS** after it.

The P wave of a blocked PAC usually sits **on top of the preceding T wave**, which deforms it. If you
do not look carefully, the pause looks like a sinus pause or an AV block. Before diagnosing either,
**examine every T wave** in the pause.

In this scenario every third beat is a blocked PAC.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("pac_blocked")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "A pause occurs after a T wave that looks pointed or notched. What should you suspect?",
                choices: [
                    "A blocked premature atrial contraction hidden in the T wave",
                    "Complete heart block",
                    "A ventricular premature beat",
                    "Sinoatrial exit block",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "A P wave landing on the T wave distorts it. Because the AV node is still refractory, no QRS follows. This is the commonest cause of unexplained pauses in an otherwise normal rhythm.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "A fast ectopic atrial focus",
            markdown: r#"
If an area of atrial muscle fires repeatedly and faster than the sinus node, it takes over the
rhythm: **atrial tachycardia**. The atria are driven from the new focus, so the **P wave has a
different shape** from the sinus P wave. At 150 bpm the rhythm is regular and the QRS is narrow.

Unlike the re-entry tachycardias, it does not start by a premature beat and stop in a single
beat. It may start slowly (a "warm-up") and slow before it stops. Causes include digoxin toxicity,
lung disease, and structural heart disease.

Compare the P wave with the sinus P wave on the page about normal rhythm.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("atrial_tachycardia_150")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "What distinguishes the P wave of atrial tachycardia from a sinus P wave?",
                choices: [
                    "Its shape, because it starts from a different place in the atria",
                    "It is always absent",
                    "It always follows the QRS",
                    "It is always wider than the QRS",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The P wave shape depends on the path of atrial activation. An ectopic focus gives a different path and therefore a different P wave from the sinus node.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Sick sinus syndrome: fast and slow",
            markdown: r#"
Sometimes the problem is not one fast focus but a **diseased sinus node**. In the **tachycardia-
bradycardia syndrome** the heart alternates between spells of fast atrial rhythm and **slow rates or
long pauses**, often with an escape beat from the junction.

Treating the fast phase with drugs that slow the heart can make the slow phase worse, so these
patients frequently need a **pacemaker** first, and then drugs for the fast phase.

The simulator alternates between 130 and 38 bpm every six seconds.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sick_sinus_tachy_brady")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Why do patients with tachycardia-bradycardia syndrome often need a pacemaker before rate-slowing drugs?",
                choices: [
                    "The drugs would deepen the slow phase and its pauses",
                    "The drugs have no effect on the fast phase",
                    "A pacemaker cures the sinus node",
                    "The drugs make the QRS wide",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Beta-blockers and calcium-channel blockers suppress the sinus and AV nodes. In a node that already pauses, they lengthen the pauses. A pacemaker provides a safe floor for the rate.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Atrial flutter: the sawtooth",
            markdown: r#"
**Atrial flutter** is one large re-entrant circuit, usually around the tricuspid valve. It turns about
**300 times a minute**, so the atria are activated every 200 ms.

The result is a **sawtooth** baseline with no flat line between waves: **negative in II, III and
aVF, positive in V1**. Here the AV node passes every fourth wave, so the ventricles beat at a calm
75 bpm and you can see the sawtooth clearly.

Count the flutter waves between two QRS complexes in lead II.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("flutter_4to1")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "At 4:1 conduction with a flutter rate of 300/min, what is the ventricular rate?",
                choices: [
                    "75 bpm",
                    "150 bpm",
                    "300 bpm",
                    "40 bpm",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The ventricles follow every fourth wave: 300 divided by 4 is 75 bpm. A normal rate does not rule out flutter, so check the baseline.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Flutter at 2:1: the rate of 150",
            markdown: r#"
A healthy AV node usually conducts **every second** flutter wave. That makes a regular narrow
tachycardia at about **150 bpm**, and half the flutter waves are hidden in the ST-T segment.

This is one of the most often missed rhythms: it looks like sinus tachycardia. Be suspicious of
any regular narrow tachycardia that stays **fixed near 150 bpm**.

The simulator gives **adenosine** after eight seconds: the AV block deepens for a few seconds and the
sawtooth shows. It does not stop the flutter, because the circuit is in the atrium.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("flutter_2to1")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
                Ui(WaitForEvent(ElapsedMs(8000.0))),
                Sim(Drug(name: "adenosine", dose: 1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Why does adenosine show the flutter waves without ending the flutter?",
                choices: [
                    "It blocks the AV node only, and the circuit lies in the atrium",
                    "It removes the T waves",
                    "It speeds up the atria",
                    "It stops the sinus node",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Adenosine produces a few seconds of AV block. Fewer QRS complexes means the sawtooth can be seen, but the atrial circuit does not depend on the AV node and carries on.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Flutter with variable block",
            markdown: r#"
When the AV ratio keeps changing the ventricular rhythm becomes **irregular**, and at a glance it
looks like atrial fibrillation.

The difference is in the baseline. **Flutter waves look alike and keep a fixed spacing**, whatever
the QRS complexes are doing. The f waves of fibrillation vary in size, shape and spacing. Here the
ratio shifts between 2:1 and 3:1.

Open the 12-lead view and look at the waves in II and V1.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("flutter_variable")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(TwelveLead)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "An irregular narrow tachycardia has identical, evenly spaced atrial waves at 300/min. What is it?",
                choices: [
                    "Atrial flutter with variable block",
                    "Atrial fibrillation",
                    "Sinus arrhythmia",
                    "Multifocal PVCs",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Regular identical atrial waves mean an organised circuit, even when the ventricular rhythm is irregular. In AF the atrial activity itself is irregular.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Atrial fibrillation",
            markdown: r#"
In **atrial fibrillation (AF)** the atria no longer contract as one: many small wavelets wander
through them, 300 to 600 times a minute. The ECG shows:

* **no P waves**, only a wavy, uneven baseline (best seen in V1 and lead II),
* an **irregularly irregular** ventricular rhythm: the R–R intervals have no pattern,
* usually a narrow QRS.

AF is the commonest sustained arrhythmia and multiplies the risk of stroke about five times,
because blood stagnates in the atria.

This scenario uses the re-entry model of the atria. It is much heavier to compute: the
simulation may run **slower than real time**, and the rhythm starts about two seconds after you open
the page, with a short burst of pacing from the pulmonary veins.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(StartReentry("af_persistent")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which two findings define atrial fibrillation on the ECG?",
                choices: [
                    "No P waves and an irregularly irregular ventricular rhythm",
                    "A P wave before every QRS and a regular rhythm",
                    "A sawtooth pattern with a fixed ratio",
                    "A wide QRS and a regular rate of 40 bpm",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Absent P waves with irregularly irregular R-R intervals are the two defining features of AF. A regular rhythm with a P wave before each QRS is sinus rhythm.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Why is the ventricular rhythm irregular?",
            markdown: r#"
The atria send hundreds of impulses per minute to the AV node. The node cannot conduct them all: it
passes **only some**, and each blocked impulse still leaves the node partly recovered or partly
refractory (*concealed conduction*), so the next one finds it in a different state.

The result is an **irregularly irregular ventricular response**. In untreated AF it is often
100 to 160 bpm. **Rate control** drugs (beta-blockers, diltiazem, verapamil, digoxin) act on the AV
node to slow it; **rhythm control** tries to restore sinus rhythm.

Count the QRS complexes in a ten-second stretch and multiply by six to estimate the ventricular rate.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(StartReentry("af_persistent")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(TwelveLead)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which part of the heart protects the ventricles from the full atrial rate in AF?",
                choices: ["The AV node", "The sinus node", "The His bundle only", "The Purkinje fibres only"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The AV node conducts only some of the atrial impulses, because of its refractory period and concealed conduction. Drugs that slow the node reduce the ventricular rate.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Fibrillation or flutter?",
            markdown: r#"
Here is atrial fibrillation drawn from the timing model, which runs in real time: **no P waves**,
a baseline of **irregular f waves**, and an **irregularly irregular** QRS at about 100 bpm.

Compare it with the flutter pages. The baseline is the thing to look at: here no two waves are
alike and there is no repeating spacing. The 3-D heart shows random wavelets in both atria,
with no common beat.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("atrial_fibrillation")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "What separates the baseline of AF from the baseline of flutter?",
                choices: [
                    "f waves vary in size, shape and spacing; flutter waves are alike and evenly spaced",
                    "f waves are larger",
                    "f waves are present only after the QRS",
                    "There is no difference",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Fibrillation has no organised circuit, so its waves are irregular in every way. Flutter is a regular circuit, so its waves repeat.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Electrical cardioversion",
            markdown: r#"
If AF has to be stopped, a **synchronised shock** can reset the heart. The energy is typically
**120 to 200 J** (biphasic). All atrial muscle is depolarised at once, the wavelets die out, and the
sinus node can take over.

Before an elective cardioversion in AF lasting **more than 48 hours** (or of unknown duration), the
patient needs **anticoagulation**, because the atria may contain clots that the return of
contraction could dislodge. In an unstable patient the shock is given without delay.

The simulator waits about 15 seconds, so you can see the fibrillation start, and then delivers a
shock. Success is a matter of probability, as in real life: if it fails, press **Replay**.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(StartReentry("af_persistent")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
                Ui(WaitForEvent(ElapsedMs(15000.0))),
                Sim(Shock(joules: 150.0, synchronised: true)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "A patient has had AF for 5 days and is stable. What must happen before an elective cardioversion?",
                choices: [
                    "Anticoagulation, because of the risk of atrial clots",
                    "Nothing, shock at once",
                    "A pacemaker",
                    "Adenosine",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "After more than 48 hours of AF, thrombus may have formed in the left atrial appendage. Restoring atrial contraction can dislodge it and cause a stroke, so the patient needs anticoagulation first (or an imaging check).",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Cardioverting flutter",
            markdown: r#"
Flutter needs **less energy** than fibrillation: guidelines suggest starting from about 70 to 120 J
biphasic, **synchronised** with the R wave. An organised circuit is easy to interrupt.

After eight seconds the simulator delivers a synchronised shock of 100 J. The sawtooth should
stop and sinus rhythm return. If the shock fails, press **Replay**.

Stroke prevention is the same as for AF: flutter carries an embolic risk too. In the longer term
**catheter ablation of the cavotricuspid isthmus** cures most typical flutter.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("flutter_2to1")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
                Ui(WaitForEvent(ElapsedMs(8000.0))),
                Sim(Shock(joules: 100.0, synchronised: true)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which shock is the right one for stable-but-symptomatic flutter being cardioverted?",
                choices: [
                    "A synchronised shock at a modest energy",
                    "An unsynchronised shock at the maximum energy",
                    "No shock works in flutter",
                    "A shock timed to the T wave",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Flutter has a clear R wave, and synchronising avoids the vulnerable T wave. Flutter usually responds to modest energy.",
            )),
        ),
    ],
)
